WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:04.080
Tucked away in a cupboard somewhere in the medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.640
department of the University of Sao&nbsp;
Paulo is a 40-year-old human skeleton.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.960
To the trained eye, the bones offer a window into&nbsp;
their former owner’s life - a hip fracture points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.480
to an old motorbike accident and a tiny hole in&nbsp;
the left cheekbone hints at severe sinusitis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:27.240
That aside, it's pretty much a standard issue&nbsp;
human skeleton, one that's helped hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.460
Brazilian forensic science students to better&nbsp;
understand how to identify human remains.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:41.220
But this apparently unassuming human skeleton&nbsp;
hides one hell of a secret. Because in life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:45.600
it belonged to one of the most evil&nbsp;
human beings ever to walk the earth.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:51.060
Now I admit there's a fair amount of hyperbole&nbsp;
going around on YouTube these days, so I'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:57.000
forgive you for thinking I'm exaggerating here&nbsp;
for effect. But trust me, this is not clickbait.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.880
You see, the classroom skeleton at Sao&nbsp;
Paolo University once belonged to a nazi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.100
war criminal who was personally responsible&nbsp;
for the deaths of an estimated 400,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:16.740
people. That on its own is mind bogglingly&nbsp;
evil, but it gets even worse. Because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:23.520
man *also* used his position as a doctor working&nbsp;
at the notorious Auschwitz concentration camp to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.900
undertake some of the most brutal,&nbsp;
depraved experiments ever carried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:33.780
out in the name of science - experiments&nbsp;
that used live humans as their subjects.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:40.380
This nazi mass murderer was called Josef&nbsp;
Mengele, but the inmates at Auschwitz had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:46.560
another, more fitting name for him -&nbsp;
the Angel of Death. This is his story.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:11.220
Delving into the early years of history's&nbsp;
most twisted individuals often turns up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:16.140
tales of hardship and abuse, but that&nbsp;
wasn't the case for young Josef Mengele.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:22.740
He was born in the picturesque Bavarian&nbsp;
town of Günzberg on the 16th March 1911,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:28.980
and his father was the founder and owner of&nbsp;
a successful farming equipment manufacturer.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:34.440
As a lad, Josef was one of those absolute&nbsp;
bastard's who seem to be good at everything - he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:40.080
was a strong student and capable sportsman with&nbsp;
an interest in the arts, and combined with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.760
good looks and natural charm, he enjoyed no&nbsp;
shortage of interest from the opposite sex.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:50.700
He excelled in further education, too, studying&nbsp;
philosophy in Munich, Medicine in Frankfurt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:57.480
and eventually earning himself a PhD in&nbsp;
Anthropology. In 1937 he got a job at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:04.500
Institute for Hereditary Biology and Racial&nbsp;
Hygiene in Frankfurt. There, he worked under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:11.460
Otmar Freiherr von Verschuer, a Dutch nobleman&nbsp;
and scientist with an interest in eugenics and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:18.300
a belief in forcibly sterilising people with&nbsp;
‘undesirable’ genetic traits. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:25.800
he was an arsehole, and Mengele quickly bought&nbsp;
into his arseholy ideas, particularly the ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:31.920
about a racial hierarchy that put people like&nbsp;
him on top, and everyone else on the bottom.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.740
A man with Mengele's scientific interests&nbsp;
was a natural fit for the racist ideology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.960
of the Nazi Party, and he became&nbsp;
a member in 1937, signing up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.380
Hitler's personal paramilitary&nbsp;
- the SS - the following year.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:51.900
Those were happy times for Mengele, and he&nbsp;
married his first wife - Irene - in July&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:57.420
1939. When the Second World War kicked off&nbsp;
two months later, Mengele volunteered for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:02.640
medical service with the Waffen-SS, and by&nbsp;
all accounts he was an excellent soldier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:07.260
serving primarily in France and Russia&nbsp;
and winning several medals in the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:12.120
including an Iron Cross First Class for&nbsp;
rescuing two men from a burning tank.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:19.200
His time on the front lines came to an end in&nbsp;
1942 when he was wounded in battle, but a man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:25.680
with a mind like Mengele’s still had his uses,&nbsp;
and in early 1943 he was transferred to Auschwitz.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:30.420
He was given the role of camp physician,&nbsp;
though that title was so inappropriate it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:36.540
almost laughable. Nazi doctors like Mengele never&nbsp;
treated the inmates, who were simply dispatched to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:42.060
the gas chambers when they fell seriously ill.&nbsp;
No, Mengele's duties at Auschwitz had nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:47.400
to do with patient care - in fact, they'd&nbsp;
have given Hippocrates of Kos an aneurysm.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:53.640
As well as being a place to commit mass murder&nbsp;
on a scale never before seen in history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.640 --> 00:06:00.120
concentration camps like Auschwitz were also&nbsp;
used for forced labour. And so the first thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:05.160
to do when a new trainload of inmates arrived&nbsp;
was determine who among them was fit enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:11.520
work. This process was known as 'Selection,' and&nbsp;
it was the job of Nazi doctors to make the call.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:17.040
It's estimated that at least 75% of all the&nbsp;
people who arrived at Auschwitz - including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:22.680
almost all children, pregnant women, and the&nbsp;
elderly - were immediately deemed unfit for work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:28.560
and sent straight to the gas chambers. To put that&nbsp;
into context, it’s thought that close to a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:34.560
people were murdered as soon as they arrived&nbsp;
at the camp. Under Mengele’s direct orders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:41.040
400,000 souls - including tens of thousands&nbsp;
of children - were sent to the gas chambers.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:48.000
There are reports that some SS doctors found&nbsp;
this task somewhat unpleasant - I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.160
you'd bloody hope so - but not&nbsp;
Mengele: he relished it. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:58.800
he enjoyed his work so much he was regularly&nbsp;
seen smiling and whistling on the job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.420
and he'd even turn up to help out&nbsp;
when he wasn't officially on shift.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:10.260
But it turned out there was an ulterior motive for&nbsp;
all that overtime. You see, Mengele had realised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:17.880
Auschwitz was the perfect place to continue his&nbsp;
research into the empirical superiority of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:26.160
Aryan race. In the eyes of the Nazis, inmates at&nbsp;
the camp weren't truly human. And that opened up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:33.360
certain... opportunities: using a grant secured&nbsp;
by his old mentor Arsehole Freiherr von Verschuer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.940
Mengele built a lab at Auschwitz that would&nbsp;
use the camp's inmates as its test subjects.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:45.300
That's why he was so keen to take part&nbsp;
in Selections - they gave him an excuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:53.280
to scan the crowds for anyone with an obvious&nbsp;
genetic abnormality he could study - club feet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:58.560
cleft palettes, people with dwarfism or&nbsp;
hunchbacks - Mengele collected them all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.280
for his own private menagerie of genetic oddities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:09.660
But there was something he prized even more&nbsp;
highly than quirky genes: identical twins.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:15.000
So-called 'twin studies' remain an&nbsp;
important tool in science even today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:20.940
because identical twins have identical&nbsp;
DNA (well almost identical, but that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:26.520
a topic for another video). Some scientists&nbsp;
believe this clone-like property makes twins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:31.800
invaluable for studying the age old&nbsp;
question of 'nature versus nurture.'

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:38.640
Mengele felt the same way, and he planned&nbsp;
to use his lab to prove that genes - namely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:45.000
Aryan ones - were the deciding factor in&nbsp;
the development of desirable traits or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:50.820
‘phenotypes.’ He also hoped to find a way to&nbsp;
help German mothers give birth to strong Aryan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:58.740
twins of their own to more quickly repopulate&nbsp;
the world according to Hitler’s mad ideal.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:05.640
Think of the scariest, most disturbing&nbsp;
scenes from the most extreme horror films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:12.060
you've seen and they won't even come close to&nbsp;
the living nightmare that was Mengele's lab.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:16.920
We'll likely never know the full extent of&nbsp;
what went on in those blood-soaked corridors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:21.120
because the mad doctor himself destroyed&nbsp;
most of his records when it became clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:26.100
Germany would lose the war, but what&nbsp;
we do know is almost beyond belief.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:31.620
Some of Mengele’s twins had their eyes&nbsp;
injected with chemicals in an attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:37.680
to turn them Aryan blue, while others were&nbsp;
made to endure extensive surgeries without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:44.340
anaesthesia, including amputations, organ&nbsp;
removal, and castration. In some cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:49.080
one twin was deliberately infected with&nbsp;
a disease like typhus or tuberculosis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:54.180
When it killed them, the surviving&nbsp;
twin would be murdered too so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:58.500
a comparative autopsy could be carried&nbsp;
out to study the effects of the disease.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:04.020
Mengele claimed to be a man of science&nbsp;
looking for answers to important questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:11.520
but that was just a mask. Sometimes that facade&nbsp;
slipped entirely, revealing the monster beneath.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:17.160
One Auschwitz inmate saw Mengele throw a&nbsp;
newborn baby off a camp roof, and another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:23.400
saw him kill 14 children in a single night by&nbsp;
injecting chloroform directly into their hearts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:30.720
one after the other. Another survivor described&nbsp;
how Mengele once sewed two children together in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:36.120
an attempt to create artificially conjoined&nbsp;
twins. They died of gangrene two days later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:42.660
This wasn’t science, it was murder of&nbsp;
the most sadistic variety imaginable.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:49.500
Of the 1500 sets of twins taken into Mengele's&nbsp;
lab, just 200 individuals are thought to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:55.380
come out alive. Those that died were dissected,&nbsp;
with various parts of their anatomy removed for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.380 --> 00:11:00.180
further study. Of particular interest&nbsp;
were the eyes, and one entire wall of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:05.760
the lab was full of them, each pinned in&nbsp;
place like a butterfly in a display case.

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:12.540
Several survivors report that, outside of his lab,&nbsp;
Mengele often showed kindness to his subjects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:19.620
insisting that they call him Uncle and handing out&nbsp;
candy and milk to the younger children. Sweets in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:25.500
the morning, scalpels in the evening - what kind&nbsp;
of person is capable of something like that?

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:32.340
This utter depravity continued unchecked&nbsp;
for 2 years until the Angel of Death was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:38.400
forced to leave Auschwitz just before its&nbsp;
liberation by the Soviets in January 1945.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:43.380
He fled to Czechoslovakia, where he was&nbsp;
eventually captured by the Americans.

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:48.840
I wish I could tell you that at this point&nbsp;
in the story, Josef Mengele finally got what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:53.520
he deserved. That he was put on trial in&nbsp;
Nuremberg and left to rot in the deepest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.520 --> 00:12:00.240
darkest dungeon for the rest of his days. Sadly,&nbsp;
that isn't quite how this story goes. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.740
that is the exact *opposite,* of how this&nbsp;
story goes. But I'm getting ahead of myself.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:07.920
Just one month after he was taken into custody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:13.380
Mengele, one of the most wanted individuals&nbsp;
on planet earth, was released as a free man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:20.940
As for why - he simply hadn't been correctly&nbsp;
identified. There were so many nazi war criminals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:25.920
knocking around at the time that the Allies&nbsp;
were having trouble keeping up, and despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:31.920
initially being registered under his real name,&nbsp;
somehow Mengele slipped through the cracks.

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:37.680
He wasted no time going to ground, working as a&nbsp;
farmhand in deepest Bavaria for 4 years before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:42.780
eventually escaping Germany altogether&nbsp;
along one of the so-called ratlines that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:46.680
provided escape routes for Nazi war&nbsp;
criminals, mostly to South America.

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:52.680
Mengele was able to convince the Red Cross to&nbsp;
issue him a passport under the name Helmut Gregor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:57.540
and he used it to make his way to&nbsp;
the Argentine capital Buenos Aires.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:01.860
To begin with he was sheltered by nazi&nbsp;
sympathisers, but eventually he bought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:06.600
himself a swanky apartment in the city&nbsp;
centre. To give you an idea of just how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:12.420
unconcerned he was about the possibility of his&nbsp;
past catching up with him, Mengele applied for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:18.420
Argentine citizenship under his real name, and&nbsp;
he soon got a job working as a regional sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:26.460
representative for - and I'm not making this up&nbsp;
- Karl Mengele &amp; Sons, his father's own company.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:31.440
By the late 50s, he'd apparently decided&nbsp;
the folks back in Europe must have put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:35.400
that whole Holocaust business behind&nbsp;
them, because he flew back to Germany&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:39.660
to visit family and catch up with some old&nbsp;
buddies on a skiing holiday in Switzerland.

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:44.940
Quick reminder here that we're talking about a&nbsp;
man who was personally responsible for *400,000*&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:49.020
murders. And he was just...&nbsp;
going about his business as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:51.180
though the Second World War had never happened.

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:55.140
If you're wondering how the hell that&nbsp;
was possible - how this despicable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:59.940
human being somehow got away Scott free&nbsp;
- the answer is actually quite simple:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:04.980
the rest of the world thought he was dead. Mengele&nbsp;
hadn't been seen since he slipped through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:11.340
American's fingers in 1945, and his own family&nbsp;
had gone on record to confirm he'd been killed.

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:15.960
If he hadn't been so monumentally&nbsp;
stupid as to use his real name in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:19.800
his new life in South America, there's a&nbsp;
good chance the wider world would never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:24.600
have realised he survived the war.&nbsp;
Thankfully he *was* that stupid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:31.560
and that was all it took for famous nazi&nbsp;
hunter Simon Wiesenthal to get on his trail.

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:36.300
In the aftermath of the war, Mengele's&nbsp;
wife Irene hadn't fancied relocating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:38.940
to South America with her mass-murdering husband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:45.600
and in 1954 the two were officially divorced. Not&nbsp;
that Mengele minded - he took the opportunity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:51.000
marry his dead brother's widow. He really&nbsp;
was a classy guy wasn't he. But of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:55.920
marriages and divorces leave a pretty bloody&nbsp;
obvious paper trail. And not only did those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.920 --> 00:15:02.280
documents confirm Mengele was still alive, they&nbsp;
also listed his home address in Buenos Aires.

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:07.500
Having realised Mengele was still&nbsp;
at large, Simon Wiesenthal convinced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:12.060
the West German government to issue a&nbsp;
warrant for his arrest. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:18.600
when police arrived at Mengele's apartment in June&nbsp;
1959, the Angel of Death had already taken flight.

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:23.220
As it happens, Mengele had no idea the&nbsp;
authorities were onto him - he'd gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:27.660
to ground for entirely different reasons.&nbsp;
Along with working for the family firm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:31.800
Mengele had been doing a bit of illegal doctoring&nbsp;
on the side, including performing backstreet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:37.440
abortions. When one of those procedures&nbsp;
went wrong and a young girl died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:43.860
the local press started sniffing around.&nbsp;
They say no publicity is bad publicity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:49.920
but I'm pretty sure that doesn't apply&nbsp;
when you're a mass-murdering shit face..

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:53.880
Worried that the attention might lead&nbsp;
to some very uncomfortable questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:59.280
Mengele swapped Buenos Aires for a&nbsp;
remote farm in Southern Paraguay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:07.260
where he gained citizenship in 1959 under&nbsp;
the incredibly clever alias 'José Mengele.'

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:13.080
Despite this frankly embarrassing attempt at&nbsp;
covering his tracks, Mengele proved surprisingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:18.480
hard to pin down in Paraguay, and neither Simon&nbsp;
Wiesenthal nor Mossad - the Israeli Secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:23.880
Service - were able to bring him to justice.&nbsp;
Thankfully, their combined efforts hadn't gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:29.940
unnoticed by Mengele himself - he now knew he was&nbsp;
a wanted man, and that knowledge changed his life.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:36.420
He was forced to relocate once again, this time&nbsp;
to Brazil, and the spectre of capture hung over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:42.240
him constantly, an incessant worry that frayed his&nbsp;
nerves and eventually began to impact his health.

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:47.580
By the early 1970s he'd developed a nervous&nbsp;
habit of chewing the tips of the moustache&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:52.800
he'd grown to alter his appearance, a&nbsp;
compulsion that got so bad a giant ball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:58.620
of beard-hair formed in his intestines, almost&nbsp;
killing him. Almost murdering himself with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:03.720
own moustache seems to have shaken Mengele up&nbsp;
a bit, because he briefly considered heading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:08.700
back to Germany to seek medical help.&nbsp;
The trouble was, he couldn't afford it.

00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:13.260
By this time there now multiple bounties&nbsp;
on Mengele's head, which made getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:19.320
work risky, and his support network of nazi&nbsp;
sympathisers had begun to dwindle. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:23.220
several of those who still knew his real&nbsp;
identity had started to blackmail him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:26.280
threatening to reveal his&nbsp;
secret if he didn’t pay up.

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:29.100
Mengele kept a diary in the&nbsp;
later years of his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:34.560
and the entries within reveal a man&nbsp;
who was depressed, poor, paranoid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:42.120
pessimistic and constantly poorly. It's far from&nbsp;
the justice he deserved, but it's something.

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:47.940
Sadly, the diary also revealed that Mengele&nbsp;
never felt remotely remorseful for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:54.360
unspeakable things he did, nor did his belief in&nbsp;
the Nazi ideology ever waver, even until the end.

00:17:55.020 --> 00:18:01.320
Speaking of ends, Mengele's came in 1979 when&nbsp;
he suffered a stroke while swimming in the sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:06.480
not far from Sao Paulo. He survived the&nbsp;
stroke, but not the subsequent influx&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:11.340
of several litres of seawater into his&nbsp;
lungs. He drowned on the 7th February,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:16.800
almost 35 years after leaving Auschwitz,&nbsp;
and was buried under a false name.

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:22.680
It wasn't until 1985 that the rest of the&nbsp;
world learned of his fate. West Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:27.360
Israel, and the U.S.A. had finally begun&nbsp;
to make a combined effort to find him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:31.980
and the operation eventually led them&nbsp;
to a cemetery on the outskirts of Sao&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:36.540
Paulo and a simple grave marked&nbsp;
with the name Wolfgang Gerhard.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:41.460
The bones within were exhumed and a forensic&nbsp;
analysis concluded that they likely belong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:46.560
to Josef Mengele, but doubts remained. At the&nbsp;
time the bounty on Mengele's head stood at 3.4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:51.420
million US dollars, and some people believed&nbsp;
the body in the grave was a red herring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:54.840
planted there by his family to&nbsp;
throw investigators off the scent.

00:18:55.500 --> 00:19:00.780
That theory was put to bed in 1992 when&nbsp;
DNA testing confirmed the bones belonged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:04.260
to Mengele. For obvious reasons,&nbsp;
his family weren't keen to have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:09.300
remains returned to Germany, which is how&nbsp;
they ended up locked away in a box in Sao&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:14.820
Paolo's Institute for Forensic Medicine.&nbsp;
They remained there for some 25 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:19.380
before a faculty member realised he&nbsp;
could put them to use as a teaching aid.

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:25.740
We're used to the idea that true, unfiltered&nbsp;
evil is something that can't be concealed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:32.580
Whether it's the terrifying clown makeup of John&nbsp;
Wayne Gacy or the cold, dead eyes of Ed Gein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:39.720
we assume that a rotten inside will eventually be&nbsp;
visible on the outside, too. But that wasn't the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:45.420
case with Josef Mengele. This could be a picture&nbsp;
of your mate's favourite grandfather (well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:51.780
minus the SS insignia). He was handsome, he&nbsp;
was charming, he smiled openly and often.

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:59.700
And yet, this man wasn't merely a wolf in sheep's&nbsp;
clothing - he was a monster draped in the skin of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:04.440
a man. During the Second World War he committed&nbsp;
some of the most unfathomably evil acts of any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:10.740
human being that's ever lived, and he did it all&nbsp;
with a smile and a sweetie. For my money, the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.740 --> 00:20:16.020
that he was never made to pay for those crimes&nbsp;
remains one of the biggest travesties of our time.

00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:18.120
Thanks for watching.

